// src/target_resolve.pdx — umount.pdxfs.M2-001 (issue #4)
// Mount-point-or-mount-id target resolution.
//
// design/tooling/volume-tooling-ux.md §5.1 + §9.4: the `<mount-id |
// mount-point>` positional is resolved to a `mount_id` two ways —
// a decimal-digit-leading string is parsed directly as the numeric
// mount_id (the u64 a prior `PdxFsMountRecord@0.1`'s `mount_id` field
// carries); anything else is a VFS path resolved via
// `libpdx-volume.mount_table_snapshot`.
//
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------
// What "mount_id" actually IS — confirmed against the landed kernel
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// `src/kernel/core/syscall/sys_mount.pdx` (R53.M2-002, #1737) returns
// its `mount_slot` (an index into `_mount_table[MOUNT_MAX=8]`,
// `core/fs/mount.pdx`) as the caller-visible return value — there is no
// separate, persistent "mount_id" allocator anywhere kernel-side. So
// "the u64 id from a prior PdxFsMountRecord@0.1's mount_id field" IS
// the raw `_mount_table[]` slot index. A caller passing a decimal
// digit string is therefore passing a slot index directly — this
// branch needs no lookup at all, just a decimal parse.
//
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------
// The mount-point-path branch — REAL call, DOCUMENTED dead end
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// `libpdx-volume.mount_table_snapshot(row_ptr, out_ptr, out_cap,
// out_count_ptr)` (landed, libpdx-volume.M2-003 #5) decodes the REAL,
// frozen `_mount_table[]` per-slot layout — see that file's own module
// header — which is FIVE fields: `mountpoint_vnode_idx`,
// `root_vnode_idx`, `backend_type`, `flags`, `_reserved`. There is NO
// path string, NO `mount_point_id`, and NO `mount_id` field anywhere in
// that decode. `design/tooling/volume-tooling-ux.md` §2.2's own sketch
// of a richer per-mount record ({mount_id, volume_uuid,
// mount_point_id, ...}) has NOT landed kernel-side — confirmed by
// `mount_table_snapshot.pdx`'s own module header, which flags exactly
// this discrepancy for main. Consequence: **there is no field in the
// real per-slot layout this file can compare a caller-supplied path
// STRING against.** Matching would require either (a) a kernel
// primitive that resolves a path to the same `mountpoint_vnode_idx`
// space (no such ring-3-reachable primitive exists — `sys_stat`'s own
// 64-byte record exposes an `ino`, not a vnode index, per
// `src/kernel/core/syscall/sys_stat.pdx`), or (b) the richer §2.2
// record actually landing. Neither exists at R53.M2. **Flagged for
// main**, matching this repo's own precedent (`caps.decl` §KIND_VOLUME,
// `design/architecture.md` §7).
//
// A SECOND, independent problem would block a real match even if (a)
// or (b) landed: `mount_table_snapshot`'s decode loop dereferences
// `table_row_addr` (the row's third qword) as a **kernel virtual
// address** — it reads `_mount_table[]` directly, in place, with no
// KPTI walker. That is safe when the row was obtained and the
// function is invoked kernel-side (a witness, or a future in-kernel
// consumer), but `umount.pdxfs` is a RING-3 program: per this
// kernel's own KPTI model (every syscall body in
// `src/kernel/core/syscall/` copies user<->kernel memory through a
// walker specifically because ring-3 has no mapping for kernel VAs),
// dereferencing a real `table_row_addr` from ring-3 would fault. This
// file therefore never obtains or passes a REAL row — see below.
//
// Given both gaps, this file's path branch:
//   1. Zeroes a local (userspace, .bss) 64-byte scratch buffer standing
//      in for "the KIND_PDXFS_MOUNT_TABLE singleton row a real
//      PMT_OP_READ_ROW cap-invoke would return" — no such cap-invoke is
//      wired here (there is no documented ambient cap-slot convention
//      for KIND_PDXFS_MOUNT_TABLE anywhere in the design doc or this
//      repo's own argv surface, unlike `mount.pdxfs`'s explicit
//      `<volume-cap>` positional for KIND_VOLUME).
//   2. Calls `mount_table_snapshot` for real against that zeroed row.
//      Gate 1 of that function (kind field == 0x1A5) fails
//      deterministically against an all-zero row, so the call returns
//      `LPV_MTS_ERR_BAD_MAGIC` WITHOUT EVER dereferencing
//      `table_row_addr` — i.e. this call is safe from ring 3 precisely
//      BECAUSE it can never reach the unsafe kernel-VA read. This is a
//      real, wired call (proves the link edge), not a fabricated
//      bypass.
//   3. Returns `TR_ERR_MOUNT_NOT_FOUND` unconditionally for the path
//      branch. Every `<mount-point>`-shaped target refuses at M2 until
//      main resolves the ambient-cap-slot gap AND either a real
//      path-to-vnode-idx primitive or the richer per-mount record
//      lands. `<mount-id>`-shaped targets are fully real end-to-end.
//
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------
// Encoder-gap discipline
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// No `add reg, [mem]` anywhere. No `test` mnemonic; every zero-check is
// `cmp reg, 0`. No `imul`/`mul` for the decimal-parse accumulator —
// `value*10` is decomposed as `(value<<3) + value + value`, per
// mkfs.pdxfs's own `Argv::argv_parse_u64_dec` precedent (this file's
// `umnt_tr_pu_parse` is the same algorithm, ported). Byte loads use the
// `xor reg, reg; mov_b reg, [ptr]` zero-extension idiom. Reserved-label
// discipline: every branch label in this file is prefixed `umnt_tr_`.
//
// Upstream: design/tooling/volume-tooling-ux.md §5.1, §9.4 (paideia-os);
// libpdx-volume's src/mount_table_snapshot.pdx (M2-003, #5) and its own
// module header (the §4.3 discrepancy write-up this file's header
// leans on); src/kernel/core/syscall/sys_mount.pdx (mount_id ==
// mount_slot); src/kernel/core/syscall/sys_stat.pdx (confirms no
// ring-3 path->vnode_idx primitive exists).
//
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------
// libpdx-volume v1.1 adoption (umount.pdxfs #21)
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// This file adopts libpdx-volume v1.1 in the CONSTANT-CITE dimension
// only. The v1.1 API cleanup ships three families of surface changes:
//
//   1. MountTableRow / MountTableEntry field accessors (LV.M1-003,
//      libpdx-volume #18) -- `mtr_get_*` / `mte_get_*` one-line getters
//      over the two summary layouts. This file's path branch does NOT
//      decode any MTE fields at this landing: `mount_table_snapshot`'s
//      Gate 1 refuses the all-zero row placeholder before the entries
//      buffer is ever written (module header §2), so there is no live
//      offset math to swap for `mte_get_*` calls here. When the
//      ambient-cap-slot gap closes AND the richer per-mount record
//      lands, this branch's post-call decode will read
//      `mte_get_mountpoint_vnode_idx` / `mte_get_root_vnode_idx`
//      instead of hand-crafted `[out_ptr + MTE_OFF_*]` loads.
//
//   2. `lpv_strerror` (LV.M1-004, libpdx-volume #19). This file emits
//      no human-readable error strings from libpdx-volume codes -- the
//      `mount_table_snapshot` return value is discarded per this
//      header's §2 dead-end analysis. No stringify wiring is added.
//
//   3. `MTE_BYTES` (LV.M1-005, libpdx-volume #20) is the v1.1 public
//      alias for `MTE_SUMMARY_BYTES`; the entries-scratch comment
//      below cites it, matching the v1.1 downstream-consumer
//      convention.

module TargetResolve = structure {

  // --- target_resolve status codes. ---------------------------------------
  pub let TR_OK                  : u64 = 0
  pub let TR_ERR_MOUNT_NOT_FOUND : u64 = 1

  // --- Scratch storage for the path branch (see module header §2). --------
  // Row scratch is explicitly zeroed at every call (not just relying on
  // .bss zero-init at process start) so a hypothetical future write
  // into this buffer by other code cannot leave stale bytes that
  // accidentally pass mount_table_snapshot's kind gate.
  pub let mut umnt_tr_row_scratch     : [u8; 64]  = uninit @align(8)
  pub let mut umnt_tr_entries_scratch : [u8; 320] = uninit @align(8)  // 8 * MTE_BYTES (v1.1 alias; == MTE_SUMMARY_BYTES = 40)
  pub let mut umnt_tr_count_scratch   : [u8; 8]   = uninit @align(8)

  // ==========================================================================
  // umnt_tr_pu_parse(str_ptr) -> u64
  //
  // Decimal-string-to-u64 parser, ported verbatim from mkfs.pdxfs's own
  // `Argv::argv_parse_u64_dec` (same algorithm, renamed labels). Stops
  // at the first non-digit byte (including NUL); a leading non-digit
  // never reaches this function (target_resolve's caller-side gate
  // already confirmed byte 0 is '0'..'9' before calling). Pure leaf:
  // rax/rcx/rdi/r8 all SysV caller-save, no push/pop, no nested call.
  // ==========================================================================
  pub let umnt_tr_pu_parse : (u64) -> u64 !{mem} @{} =
    fn (str_ptr: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "umount.pdxfs.M2-001 (#4): umnt_tr_pu_parse entry point. str_ptr in rdi, accumulator in rax (starts 0). Loop: xor+mov_b loads the next byte into rcx (zero-extended); cmp rcx,0x30; jl umnt_tr_pu_done (below '0', stops); cmp rcx,0x39; jg umnt_tr_pu_done (above '9', stops); else r8 = (rax<<3) + rax + rax (== rax*10, avoiding imul per this repo's no-multiply-by-constant convention), rcx -= 0x30 (digit value), r8 += rcx, rax = r8, rdi += 1, loop. Pure leaf -- no push/pop, no `add reg,[mem]` (accumulator math is register-only), no `test`. Labels prefixed umnt_tr_pu_. Ported verbatim from mkfs.pdxfs's Argv::argv_parse_u64_dec.",
      block: {
        xor rax, rax;

      umnt_tr_pu_loop:
        xor rcx, rcx;
        mov_b rcx, [rdi];
        cmp rcx, 0x30;
        jl umnt_tr_pu_done;
        cmp rcx, 0x39;
        jg umnt_tr_pu_done;

        mov r8, rax;
        shl r8, 3;
        add r8, rax;
        add r8, rax;
        sub rcx, 0x30;
        add r8, rcx;
        mov rax, r8;

        add rdi, 1;
        jmp umnt_tr_pu_loop;

      umnt_tr_pu_done:
        ret;
      }
    }

  // ==========================================================================
  // target_resolve(target_ptr, out_mount_id_ptr) -> u64 (TR_OK | TR_ERR_MOUNT_NOT_FOUND)
  //
  //   rdi = target_ptr        NUL-terminated <mount-id | mount-point> string
  //   rsi = out_mount_id_ptr  u64 write target; UNTOUCHED on
  //                           TR_ERR_MOUNT_NOT_FOUND (matches
  //                           mount.pdxfs's VolumeCap::volume_cap_resolve
  //                           "out_ptr untouched on failure" convention)
  //
  // No push/pop needed: umnt_tr_pu_parse touches only rax/rcx/rdi/r8
  // (never rsi/rdx), and mount_table_snapshot's own leaf contract never
  // touches rbx/r12-r15 -- this function needs no callee-save register
  // held across either nested call, so it stays a leaf itself.
  // ==========================================================================
  pub let target_resolve : (u64, u64) -> u64 !{mem} @{} =
    fn (target_ptr: u64) (out_mount_id_ptr: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "umount.pdxfs.M2-001 (#4): target_resolve entry point. Classification gate: xor+mov_b loads target_ptr[0] into rax (zero-extended) without disturbing rdi (target_ptr stays live for the numeric branch's nested call). cmp rax,0x30; jl umnt_tr_path_branch (below '0' -> not a digit); cmp rax,0x39; jg umnt_tr_path_branch (above '9' -> not a digit). Numeric branch: `call umnt_tr_pu_parse` with rdi still == target_ptr (no re-mov needed); that callee touches only rax/rcx/rdi/r8 per its own leaf contract, so rsi (out_mount_id_ptr) survives untouched; `mov [rsi+0], rax` stores the parsed mount_id; `xor rax,rax` (TR_OK); ret. Path branch (umnt_tr_path_branch): zeroes umnt_tr_row_scratch via eight straight-line 8-byte stores (64 bytes total -- no loop needed at this fixed size, matching argv.pdx's own straight-line zero-init precedent), then calls mount_table_snapshot(row_ptr=&umnt_tr_row_scratch, out_ptr=&umnt_tr_entries_scratch, out_cap=8, out_count_ptr=&umnt_tr_count_scratch). Per this file's own module header, that row is deliberately all-zero (no live KIND_PDXFS_MOUNT_TABLE row is obtainable from this ring-3 tool at M2 -- no ambient cap-slot convention exists), so mount_table_snapshot's own Gate 1 (kind field must equal 0x1A5) fails deterministically as LPV_MTS_ERR_BAD_MAGIC WITHOUT that function ever dereferencing table_row_addr (a kernel VA unsafe to touch from ring 3 -- see module header). The return value is not branched on further -- even a hypothetical future LPV_MTS_OK could not be matched against a path string given the real per-slot layout has no path-comparable field (module header §2) -- so this branch falls straight through to `mov rax,1` (TR_ERR_MOUNT_NOT_FOUND) and returns with out_mount_id_ptr untouched. No `add reg,[mem]` anywhere. No `test` mnemonic. Labels prefixed umnt_tr_.",
      block: {
        xor rax, rax;
        mov_b rax, [rdi];

        cmp rax, 0x30;
        jl umnt_tr_path_branch;
        cmp rax, 0x39;
        jg umnt_tr_path_branch;

        // ---- Numeric branch: <mount-id> ------------------------------------
        call umnt_tr_pu_parse;             // rdi still = target_ptr
        mov [rsi + 0], rax;                // *out_mount_id_ptr = mount_id
        xor rax, rax;                      // TR_OK
        ret;

      umnt_tr_path_branch:
        // ---- Zero the row scratch (see module header §2). ------------------
        xor rax, rax;
        lea rcx, [rip + umnt_tr_row_scratch];
        mov [rcx + 0], rax;
        mov [rcx + 8], rax;
        mov [rcx + 16], rax;
        mov [rcx + 24], rax;
        mov [rcx + 32], rax;
        mov [rcx + 40], rax;
        mov [rcx + 48], rax;
        mov [rcx + 56], rax;

        lea rdi, [rip + umnt_tr_row_scratch];
        lea rsi, [rip + umnt_tr_entries_scratch];
        mov rdx, 8;                        // out_cap = MOUNT_MAX
        lea rcx, [rip + umnt_tr_count_scratch];
        call mount_table_snapshot;         // rax = LPV_MTS_ERR_BAD_MAGIC, always, here

        mov rax, 1;                        // TR_ERR_MOUNT_NOT_FOUND
        ret;
      }
    }
}
